foreign  cancer awareness month comes to a close  it's the last day we bring you a  reminder that it is never never the  wrong time to get screened let's say  that again never the wrong time to get  screamed screen ladies this year breast  cancer is expecting to kill more than 43  000 women in this country but early  detection is helping more women survive  the disease CBS News contributor and the  editor at large at kfs Kaiser health  news Dr Celine gounder documented her  annual exam in mammography 101 she hopes  to demystify the process and explain  again why regular screening is vital for  detecting breast cancer early  one of the awesome things about being a  45 year old woman is it's three times  the fun with cancer screening you've got  screening for breast cancer colon cancer  and cervical cancer my name is Danielle  I'm going to do your exam today I'm  going to place your breast here on the  plate just some slight compression just  enough to keep your breasts from moving  we will do this in several different  angles taking a breath hold your breath  you can put the Gown back on on the  right side and we'll do the side view  and taking a breath and hold your breath  beautiful okay  great looks good  I'm Dr Donaldson hi my name is the right  and the left and we get an image and two  views for each breast so that we really  can look at the full volume of the  tissue so these are your breasts your  mammogram more zoomed in and enlarged  and we very carefully look at really  every inch every area of the breast  looking for anything that really doesn't  belong there  and so far you look fantastic  so Celine gowner joins us here at the  table let's just get right into it you  know do you have that moment that I do  when you're sitting there waiting it's  it's the waiting for them to walk in the  room and I'm trying to read their  expression before they say either yay or  nay did you have that moment yourself as  a doctor when you're waiting to get the  news  we're glad no abnormalities were found  by the way very glad but did you do you  have that moment too or no not really I  didn't I didn't I had done a self breast  exam before going uh to the appointment  and was pretty sure I was you know low  risk and wasn't too worried I don't have  a family history but you know you never  know many women do not have a family  history something like 85 percent of  women do not have a family history when  they're diagnosed with breast cancer but  there's still so many women despite all  the statistics that say I'm not going to  get it because they're afraid of the  process or I've heard somebody say I  just don't even want to know which is  not good thinking in my opinion yeah  look a mammogram does not give you  breast cancer it's really important to  know a lot of times people will say to  me oh that test gave me such and such  disease and no it helps you find out  that you have it it helps you get care  more quickly if you have it and it's  really not a painful process it's sort  of awkward and you have your breasts  compressed between two plates but it's  not painful and it's uncomfortable I  think it depends on the technician and I  swear if men had to have their peonies  matched with way we do with our breasts  they find another way to do it but right  now but that's this is all that we have  it can be a little uncomfortable  depending on who's giving you giving it  to you but there's that is not enough  not to go and get it right it's not it's  not painful it doesn't pinch it's just  sort of awkward yeah so when should  women get a mammogram  on the organization  um their recommendations do vary um  some organizations will say every  years  across the board definitely you want to  start at age 50 and then if you have  risk factors so for example a family  history obesity if you drink heavily if  you smoke those are risk factors then  you may want to start earlier as early  as age 40. and some women get a sonogram  in addition to a mammogram what's the  difference  sonograms are used to tease out to have  a letter a better look at abnormalities  they might see on the mammogram it's  more likely that you need that in  somebody who has dense breasts younger  women do tend to have denser breasts and  there's sort of a difference in  philosophy here some doctors will say  let's go ahead and get the the mammogram  and ultrasound in the same visit so you  don't have to come back that's what my  doctor does others will say well does it  really make a difference you know does  it really save lives maybe we should  wait and see if we really need that I  want to get every test possible and  that's exactly what I do it's troubling  to me though that black women are 41  percent more likely to be diagnosed with  breast cancer than white women why is  that so therefore you die from breast  cancer yeah they're more likely to die  from breast cancer than white women and  there's this is because there are  disparities at every step of the process  so they're less likely to get their  mammogram on time they're less likely to  get the newer 3D mammograms than a 2d  mammogram so a 2d mammogram you're  basically looking at all the cross  sections of the breast flattened into  one image so 3D allows you to have a  better look then once they're diagnosed  they start treatment later and they're  less likely to get standard of care you  know the recommended treatment is it  because they don't pursue it or the  doctors don't encourage it there's a  difference some of both that's some of  both yeah then there's certainly  barriers to getting the mammogram Dr  sling grounded thank you so much Vital  Information life saving information and  you're okay we'll be right back that's  all clear that's the best news of all

